1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
When you drop user text or a fetched document straight into your prompt, you hit a problem.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Hidden inside it, an instruction can ride along, and the model might follow it.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Input sanitization is how you stop that. This lesson covers cleaning,

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
validating, and delimiting untrusted input. Let's dig in.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Let's define the pieces. Input sanitization means cleaning, validating, or clearly delimiting

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
user-supplied content before it enters the prompt. So the model treats it as data and

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
not as instructions. Untrusted input is any content your application didn't author.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
That's in-user messages. But it's also retrieved documents, web content, and tool results.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
And all of it should be treated as potentially adversarial.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Content boundaries are the line you draw between your trusted developer instructions

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
and that untrusted material, often by wrapping the outside content in labeled tags.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Hold on to those three, because everything in this lesson is really about keeping data

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
on one side of a boundary and your instructions on the other.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
So what does sanitizing actually involve? Three moves. First, you clean the input,

15
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
stripping or escaping characters that could break the structure you're building around it.

16
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Second, you validate it, checking that it matches the shape, the type,

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
and the length you expected before you trust it.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Third, you delimit it, wrapping the content so its edges are unambiguous

19
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
and nothing leaks out into your instruction space.

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Notice this is all prep work. It runs before the model call,

21
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
in your own code, on your side of the wire. When you do it well,

22
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
the model receives the payload as a block of data to work on.

23
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
It never sees a fresh command hiding in there, because you already fenced it off.

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Here's the distinction that makes all of this click. Your trusted instructions,

25
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
the system prompt, and the developer directions you wrote, define what the model should do.

26
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
You authored them, so they carry authority over the task.

27
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Untrusted data is different. It's the external text,

28
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
the user fields, the tool results the model is working on.

29
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
That material should never be read as a command. It's only there as something to process.

30
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
The whole game of sanitization is keeping those two roles from blurring together.

31
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
When untrusted data gets read as an instruction, that's the exact failure we're preventing.

32
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
So, as you build, keep asking a simple question. Is this thing telling the model what to do,

33
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
or is it just something the model should look at?

34
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
The most reliable way to delimit untrusted input is a labeled tag around it.

35
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
You open and close the untrusted block with a clear, named tag,

36
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
so the boundary is obvious to the model. Then you tell the model,

37
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
up in your instructions, that anything inside those tags is data to work on,

38
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
and never a command to follow. And you keep your own instructions

39
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
outside the tags, never mixed into the same block.

40
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The reason this works is that it gives the model a visible edge.

41
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
On one side is the material it reads. On the other is the direction it obeys.

42
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Without that edge, injected text, sitting right next to your instructions,

43
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
looks just as authoritative as the instructions themselves.

44
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Placement matters as much as wrapping. The strongest move is to deliver third-party

45
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
content inside tool result blocks. The model is trained to treat instructions

46
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:29,000
that show up in tool results with skepticism, so that's the safest place for it to land.

47
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
What you want to avoid is pasting untrusted text into the system prompt,

48
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
or into a plain instruction, because there it inherits the authority of everything around it,

49
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
and keep your own directions separate. Send them in your turn,

50
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
not buried in the same block as the untrusted data.

51
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
If you mix your instructions in with the material,

52
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
you've erased the very boundary you were trying to create.

53
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Not every untrusted input is a block of free text.

54
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,000
A lot of it arrives as structured fields, a date, an amount, an ID, a category.

55
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Those get their own kind of check. For each field, you validate the type,

56
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
the range, and the allowed values before that data ever reaches the prompt.

57
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
If something fails, you reject it or escape it,

58
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
rather than passing it straight through and hoping the model sorts it out.

59
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
And like everything here, this runs on the developer's side,

60
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,000
in your code, before the model call. Picture schema validation and delimiting

61
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
working side by side. Schema validation handles the typed fields,

62
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
confirming they're what you expected. Delimiting handles the free text parts,

63
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
fencing them off. Let's place sanitization in the request

64
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
path and set expectations honestly. Where it runs, you already know,

65
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
on your side of the wire, before the call. It sits right next to schema validation

66
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
of your structured fields. One handles the free text.

67
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
The other handles the typed values. And together, they scrub the input

68
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
before it goes out. Now the honest part. This is a reduction,

69
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
not a cure. Delimiting and validating lower the odds

70
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
that a smuggled instruction gets followed, but they don't.

71
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
On their own, stop a determined injection. Someone who really wants to break out

72
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
will keep probing your wrapper. That's exactly why sanitization is never

73
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
the whole story. It's the first layer, and in the next

74
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
lesson, we stack more on top of it. Let's get concrete about how an attacker

75
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
tries to defeat your wrapper. A determined input doesn't just sit

76
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
quietly inside your tags. It tries to break out, to close your

77
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
wrapper early, and start what looks like a brand new

78
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
instruction. The defense is encoding. When you encode

79
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:48,000
untrusted content as a JSON string, the quotes, the tags,

80
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,000
the special characters, all get escaped. That escaping gives you

81
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
unambiguous delimiters, edges the attacker can't simply close

82
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,000
to slip out. The payload stays sealed inside its field,

83
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
no matter what text it contains, because the structure around it

84
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
is no longer something the content can forge.

85
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Clean, escaped edges are what keeps smuggled text

86
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
from ever posing as your own command. It's a small step in code,

87
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,000
and it shuts down a common way in. Let's recap. Sanitization means treating

88
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
untrusted input as data, not instructions. You clean it, validate

89
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
it, and delimit it before it reaches the prompt, and you

90
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:34,000
keep it out of your instruction blocks. Just remember, this lowers injection risk,

91
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
but it doesn't prevent it entirely. That covers

92
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
delimiting and validating untrusted input.

93
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
I'll see you in the next one.

